go to directory, run 
make
to get insertionSort and mergeSort executable.


insertionSort analysis: ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Worst case for swaps, see triangle -> n(n-1) / 2 , average case (half of triangle)

1.) Insertion sort.
Program runs without any bugs, however in the main file it seems that I have to enter n before integer elements when running the insertion sort file from the terminal.
e.g.

printf "5\n5\n4\n3\n2\n1\n" | ./insertionSort 
ALTERNATIVELY:  printf "[whatever]" > test.txt | ./insertionSort < test.txt


will run my program, but at the top line it will print n(num elements), then the sorted elements from least to greatest on every new line.

Analysis of Insertion Sort: (n == num elements)
Best - sorted array
Comparisons - C(n) = n - 1, only needs to compare each element to it's predecessor
Copy - M(n) = 2(n-1) , copy's one time per comparison to store key and place it back
Swaps - S(n) = 0, this algorithm doesn't use swaps
O(n) complexity

Average - random array
Comparisons - C(n) = n(n-1)/4, approximately halfway of the worst case
Copy - M(n) = n(n-1)/4 + (n-1), every comparison results in a copy, + at the end of each pass copying the key
Swaps - 0
O(n^2) Complexity

Worst - reverse sorted array
Comparisons - C(n) = n(n-1)/2 , every pass (up to n-1 passes) there is a number of comparisons equal to the pass #
Copy - M(n) = n(n-1)/2 + n-1, every comparison results in a copy + at the end of each pass copying the key
Swaps - 0
O(n^2) Complexity



mergeSort analysis: ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Code worked. Same caveat as calling insertionSort.

Analysis:
Best average and worst case are the same complexity
comparisons - O(n*logn), logn (base 2) is the amount of layers to the merge (layers of the binary tree), n comparisons per level.

copy - 2n*logn
swaps - 0
